If the neighbourhood at an index is contained in the neighbourhood at another index, and the smaller neighbourhood is consistent with the neighbourhood obtained by folding a list from the larger index, then folding the same list from the smaller index presents exactly the intersection of the smaller neighbourhood with that fold. The argument uses only the consistency gate of the step.

Induct on the list. On the empty list both folds are the starting indices, and the intersection equals the smaller neighbourhood by the inclusion. On a cons cell, decide whether the characteristic of the head equals one. If not, both steps return their accumulators and the inductive hypothesis applies to the tail. If it does, decide whether the larger accumulator is consistent with the second component of the head. If not, the larger step is idle, and the smaller step is idle as well: a consistency witness for the smaller accumulator would transport along the inclusion to a witness for the larger one. The inductive hypothesis applies. If the gate succeeds, the larger step is the intersection index. The tail fold from that index is contained in the second component by the shrinking of folds and the intersection specification. The final witness is contained in the smaller neighbourhood, in the tail fold, and therefore in the second component, so the smaller accumulator is consistent with that component and its step is the corresponding intersection. The new neighbourhoods remain nested by the two intersection specifications and the original inclusion, and the same witness lies in the intersection of the new smaller neighbourhood with the tail fold. The inductive hypothesis, the intersection specification, associativity of intersection, and containment of the tail fold in the second component give the equality.
